description:
Update the claim command reference, the coordination and troubleshooting guides, and the contributor testing guide for issue-addressed lease commands and per-issue lease records.

## Background

Docs still describe lease-id-addressed commands and an index rebuilt from the log:

- `docs/reference/claim.md:151,195-206,243-254,420-431` (lease-id arguments; the claims log as audit trail);
- `docs/reference/cli-command-grammar.md:124` (renew, heartbeat and force-evict take the lease's own id);
- `docs/how-to/multi-agent-coordination.md:129,141,171,253,317,326` ("Rebuild claims index from log");
- `docs/tutorials/parallel-work-worktrees.md:214,246,254`;
- `docs/how-to/troubleshooting.md:111-156,219-234,515-532` (rebuild the index; delete `claims.jsonl` and the index by hand);
- `docs/reference/worktree-validate.md:308,325,451,482` and `docs/reference/cli-commands.md:3465,3542` (claims-index validation, recover rebuilding the index);
- `scripts/hooks/README.md:75` (hooks read the index);
- `dev/TESTING.md:68,449,518,528,595` (rebuild property tests and claims-index validation);
- `.agents/skills/jit-manage/SKILL.md:173` (the lease record lives in `claims.index.json`).

Renew, heartbeat and force-evict now take an issue id; leases are `.git/jit/leases/<issue-id>.json`; recover cleans locks and evicts expired leases; validation does not read the claims files; the hooks ask `jit claim status`.

## Success Criteria

- [hard] REQ-01: Every listed page addresses renew, heartbeat and force-evict by issue, and none tells an operator to rebuild, delete or edit an index or the claims log.
- [hard] REQ-02: The recover and validation descriptions match their behaviour, and the hooks README names `jit claim status` as the lease source.
- [hard] REQ-03: `dev/TESTING.md` names the claim property tests and coordinator tests that exist.

## Notes

- Written against the code as it stands after the semantic-types epic (2195c98f): issue ids are `IssueId`, gate-run ids `GateRunId`, gate keys `GateKey`, lease ids `LeaseId`, events are an `Event` envelope around a tagged `EventBody`, and record paths come from `VirtualPath` constructors. Line citations name current code, which that epic retypes.
